  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Technical support for FCM system approach</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Export of cut flowers is vital for Ethiopia because of its positive socio-economic impact in terms of employment, income and much-needed foreign currency. Currently, however, the False Codling Moth (FCM) poses a key phytosanitary issue that threatens the export of cut flowers from Ethiopia to the European Union (EU) as one of the major destination markets. The EU considers this pest a serious quarantine organism that it needs to contain, for example by increasing the rate of phytosanitary inspections on exports from Ethiopia. The overall purpose of this project is to enable the Ethiopian Agricultural (Regulatory) Authority (EAA) /Ethiopian Plant Protection Organisation (NPPO-ETH) EAA/NPPO as mandated government authority in Ethiopia to reduce the risk of FCM on rose exports to the EU and in this way safeguarding the access of Ethiopian roses to the EU market. It aims to enable the EAA/NPPO in implementing a system approach for managing False Codling Moth (FCM) in rose exports to the EU and creating the conditions for effective implementation including the training of inspectors.</narrative>
  </description>
